Purpose: Record mismatches found, causes, and fixes

[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential testing: mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C program (`c_src/src/main.c`) is the ground truth. Both binaries were built
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and run as subprocesses on identical stdin, and stdout, stderr and exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m were compared byte for byte (`translation/tests/differential.rs`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` calls `scanf("%d", &x)` with `x` initialised to `0`, then branches on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `if (x)`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `x != 0` -> `good()` -> `goodG2B()` prints `04`, then `goodB2G()` evaluates
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `data < CHAR_MAX/2` as `127 < 63`, which is false, so it prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `data value is too large to perform arithmetic safely.`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `x == 0` -> `bad()` -> `data = CHAR_MAX` (127), `data * 2` is computed in `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (254) and truncated back into a signed `char`, wrapping to `-2`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printf("%02x\n", ...)` promotes that `char` to `int` and `%x` reinterprets the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bits as `unsigned int`, so the output is `fffffffe` -- eight hex digits, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   two, because `02` is only a *minimum* field width.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Exit status is always `0` on any input, and neither program ever writes to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr. Both reachable outputs are pinned to literal bytes in the test suite so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the two programs cannot drift together unnoticed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Panic/abort instead of ignoring a stdout write failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Symptom.** With stdout pointed at `/dev/full` (every write fails with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ENOSPC`), the C exited `0` with empty stderr. The Rust aborted with status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `134`, printing `thread 'main' panicked ... failed printing to stdout: No space
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   left on device` to stderr. Both stderr and exit status differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Cause.** The translation used `println!`, which panics when the underlying
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   write fails; with `panic = "abort"` in the release profile that becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `SIGABRT`. C's `printf` reports failure only through its return value and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `errno`, and this program ignores both; a failed flush inside `exit` likewise
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   does not change the status `main` returned.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Fix.** Output now goes through an explicit buffer written once with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `write_all`, and both the write and the flush results are discarded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`let _ = ...`). See `flush_stdout` in `src/main.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Covered by.** `stdout_write_error_is_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Wrong exit status on a broken stdout pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Symptom.** With stdout on a pipe whose reader had closed, the C died from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `SIGPIPE` (signal 13, shell status 141). The Rust exited 134 with a panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   message on stderr; after fix 1 alone it would have exited `0`. Either way the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   status differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Cause.** The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a write to a broken pipe returns `EPIPE` instead of killing the process. A C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program inherits the default disposition, which terminates it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` resets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` as the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   statement of `main`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Covered by.** `broken_stdout_pipe_matches`. The test closes the read end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   while the child is still blocked in `scanf` and only then sends EOF on stdin,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so the child cannot write before the pipe is broken -- the test is not racy.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. stdio buffering discipline (fixed together with 1)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `println!` writes through a `LineWriter`, flushing at every newline, whereas
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   glibc block-buffers a non-tty stdout and emits everything in a single `write`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   at exit. This is invisible when stdout is captured in full, but it changes what
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is observable when a write fails partway through. Buffering all output and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   writing once at exit reproduces C's syscall pattern as well as its bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were verified rather than fixed; they are the places a translation of this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program is most likely to go wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` crosses newlines.** Leading whitespace is skipped using C's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `isspace` set (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`), so `"\n\n\n  7"` yields 7.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fgets` would have stopped at the first newline.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Matching failure leaves `x` untouched.** `""`, `"abc"`, `"-"`, `"+"`, `"--1"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"- 1"`, `".5"`, whitespace only, a leading NUL byte, and invalid UTF-8 all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   leave `x` at its initialiser `0` and therefore reach `bad()`. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `scanf_int` returns `None` and the caller does not assign.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`int` truncation of the converted value.** glibc converts `%d` with `strtol`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   which saturates at `long` bounds, and then stores the result into an `int`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeping the low 32 bits. So `4294967296` (2^32), `68719476736` (2^36),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-4294967296`, `-9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MIN`), `-9223372036854775809`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (saturates to `LONG_MIN`) and `-99999999999999999999` all truncate to `0` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reach `bad()`, while `2147483648`, `9223372036854775808` (saturates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MAX`, low 32 bits `0xffffffff`) and `18446744073709551616` reach
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `good()`. Clamping to `int` bounds instead of truncating is a real divergence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and is caught by `int_truncation_of_the_converted_value`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Conversion stops at the first non-digit.** `"0x10"` is 0 (no hex prefix for
